WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.728 --> 00:00:10.090
My name is Jessi Dyer,

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:12.290
and I work here at L.L.Bean.

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:14.330
L.L.Bean was founded in 1912

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:17.210
by an avid outdoorsman,
Leon Leonwood Bean.

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:20.160
His first creation was
the Maine hunting shoe.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.260
That boot, most commonly
known as the L.L.Bean boot,

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:25.020
is our most iconic product.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.210
And we've made this for 110 years.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:30.020
If a customer has a
pair of well-worn boots

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:31.440
and is not ready to give them up,

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.000
we can easily resole them

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.533
and bring them back to like-new condition.

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:41.890
In order to replace and
put a new bottom on it,

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:44.640
we will be removing the
upper from this boot.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:45.780
It will go in the saw,

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.670
and we will start detaching
that with the saw and a blade.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:51.020
The boots get clamped,
they get put into the saw,

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:54.140
and it cuts right down through
the bottom of the boot.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:56.040
Once the boot comes out of the machine,

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.725
it does have a piece of the brush guard

00:00:57.725 --> 00:00:59.170
that is still attached to the leather.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:00.528
We remove this in order to have

00:01:00.528 --> 00:01:01.810
the leather all nice and clean,

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:03.751
so that it can fit on the new bottom,

00:01:03.751 --> 00:01:05.076
snug and complete.

00:01:05.076 --> 00:01:07.120
We call this "stripping the boot."

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.340
We use a flat knife as well as scissors

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:14.800
to cut the stitches away from the upper.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.022
Once this happens, we can handpick

00:01:17.022 --> 00:01:19.248
all of the stitches that remain behind.

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:29.830
In order to help us get
these stitches out easier,

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:31.810
we actually have a rub machine,

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:34.250
and this is very much
like a rotating bristle.

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:35.750
The outside of the leather boot

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:37.410
actually brushes up against that,

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:38.810
and it helps loosen the stitches

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:40.220
so that we can pull them out easier

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.770
and have a nice, clean
finish to the leather upper.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:45.680
We then pick out all of the threads

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:47.120
from the bottom part of the upper

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:48.960
so that it can be restitched

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.820
onto the new bottom nice and clean

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:54.245
and not have any straggling
pieces of thread left behind.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:02.230
We're now going to start reassembly.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:04.711
In this step, we are actually going to

00:02:04.711 --> 00:02:08.111
remove the old heel protector
and attach a new one.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:15.430
This particular boot is
looking for a brand-new bottom.

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:18.130
So we were replacing it
with the exact same bottom

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:19.100
that the customer received

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:21.110
when they purchased the boot originally.

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:23.620
In order for this to properly attach,

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:25.580
we make sure that there
is any dust or debris

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:27.150
or stitches that are all removed.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:30.332
Once that happens, we
actually apply cement

00:02:30.332 --> 00:02:32.160
to both the new bottom boot

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:33.943
as well as the leather upper.

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:40.400
Once that is complete,

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.988
we move right on to the vamping process.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:48.500
We have a vamping machine that we've done,

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.190
and this is all done by hand.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:52.410
So this particular machine will drop

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:54.790
three needles down into
the boot with stitches

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:57.891
and complete the stitching
process to the boot bottom.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:01.830
We use great care to line up the holes.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:04.330
At times, this requires
slowing down the machine

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:05.796
and really taking a fine look

00:03:05.796 --> 00:03:07.779
as to where those holes are.

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:14.470
Once we complete the vamp,

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:16.290
we move over to the packing station.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:18.700
Here is where we will
give it one final look

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:21.100
to make sure that quality is ensured.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.740
Before boxing, we make sure
that we are wiping down

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:25.683
any debris that could be left behind.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:29.250
We replace the lace and the inner sole

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:31.483
on each boot, just due to wear.

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:33.804
We try to get everything

00:03:33.804 --> 00:03:36.360
right back to its original status.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.110
Now that the boots are complete,

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:40.210
we go to packing them
up and shipping them out

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:42.217
to another happy customer.

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:51.000
I'm really happy at how
these boots came out,

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:54.109
and I cannot wait for
our customer to see them.

